Balancing double pendulum with a linear controller requires moderate gains on position, angle, and velocity terms. A negative weight on cart position (params[0]) pushes the cart toward the center when it drifts. Small negative weight on cart velocity (params[5]) damps motion. Positive weight on sin(first‑pole angle) (params[1]) generates a corrective force opposite the tilt direction, while a negative weight on sin(second‑pole angle) (params[2]) counteracts the coupled drift of the second pole. Including cosine terms (params[3], params[4]) provides offset correction for the upright orientation. Positive gains on angular velocities (params[6], params[7]) add damping to the pole motions. The bias is kept zero to avoid constant drift. These moderate, balanced weights should reduce saturation, lower jitter, and encourage longer stable periods, moving us beyond the aggressive or under‑powered policies seen so far.
